{
  // Extend the Quickshell Omarchy menu with JSONC.
  //
  // IDs are object keys. The parent is inferred from the dotted id, so
  // "personal.notes" appears under "personal", and "personal" appears on the
  // root menu. Reuse an existing id to override/extend it.
  //
  // Fields:
  //   icon        Nerd Font glyph shown in the icon column.
  //   label       Visible row title.
  //   action      Shell command to run. If omitted, the row is a submenu.
  //   target      Existing submenu id to open. Use for links/aliases.
  //   provider    Name of a shell-defined runtime row source (e.g. "fonts").
  //   aliases     alternate `omarchy menu summon <name>` routes; also searchable.
  //   description Optional subtitle and extra search text.
  //   when        Shell condition; hide row when it fails.
  //   checked     Shell condition; append ✓ when it succeeds.
  //
  // Examples:
  // "personal": {"icon":"","label":"Personal"},
  // "personal.notes": {"icon":"󰎞","label":"Notes","action":"omarchy-launch-editor ~/notes"},
  // "personal.files": {"icon":"","label":"Files","action":"uwsm-app -- nautilus ~/Documents"},
  //
  // provider can only reference row sources the shell already defines (the
  // providers map in Menu.qml). Static submenus only need dotted ids.
  //
  // Example: replace the default About action by reusing the same id. Existing
  // fields are kept unless overridden.
  // "about": {"icon":"","label":"About","action":"omarchy-launch-or-focus-tui \"zsh -c 'fastfetch; read -k 1'\""},

  // "keybindings" is the one top-level key here that is not a row id. It adds
  // keys to the ones that drive the menu, one action at a time: what you list
  // for an action joins its shipped keys rather than replacing them, and an
  // action you leave out keeps them as they are. Actions are next, prev,
  // pageNext, pagePrev, activate and back.
  //
  // A binding is one string, written the way ~/.config/hypr/bindings.lua writes
  // a Hyprland one: modifiers and key joined by "+". Spacing is up to you and
  // case does not matter, so "CTRL + J", "Ctrl+J" and "ctrl + j" are the same
  // binding. Modifiers are SUPER, CTRL, ALT and SHIFT. The key is a name like
  // DOWN, RETURN, PAGEUP or F5, or a single character.
  //
  // Binding a plain character costs you that character in search: on "back" only
  // as the first one you type, thanks to the rule below; on any other action,
  // every time. A modifier costs nothing, which is why the example uses CTRL.
  //
  // One rule about searching: a "back" binding with no modifier steps aside
  // while you have text in the search box, so LEFT moves through what you typed
  // rather than leaving the submenu. Give it a modifier -- "CTRL + H" -- and it
  // stays live while you filter.
  //
  // Escape and Delete are handled before any of this and cannot be rebound.
  // Backspace and Ctrl+U edit the search whenever it has text, so a binding on
  // either only reaches you on an empty search -- which is how the shipped
  // "back" gets Backspace. Setting an action to [] unbinds it, which is the
  // only way to take a shipped key away.
  //
  // Example: add readline and vim-style navigation. The arrow keys keep working
  // without being named here.
  // "keybindings": {
  //   "next":     ["CTRL + J", "CTRL + N"],
  //   "prev":     ["CTRL + K", "CTRL + P"],
  //   "activate": ["CTRL + L"],
  //   "back":     ["CTRL + H"],
  // },
}
